<h2>A timeline that should not exist</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>7 December 2012</strong> — Arrest under Section 302 IPC.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>14 August 2013</strong> — Conviction and life sentence by the Sessions Court.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>20 January 2018</strong> — Acquittal by the Chhattisgarh High Court in CRA No. 968 of 2013; the State files no appeal, so the acquittal becomes final.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>19 May 2025</strong> — Actual release from prison, more than seven years later.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>1 May 2026</strong> — The High Court, in a writ petition, awards Rs 2 lakh for the illegal detention.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>16 November 2026</strong> — The Supreme Court enhances the award to Rs 30 lakh.</div>
</div>

<p>Before the Supreme Court, the State explained the failure as a communication gap between the jail and the legal-aid panel lawyer. Justice Sanjeev Sachdeva, writing for the Bench, found "complete remissness" on the part of the authorities: an acquittal is not a document to be filed but an order to be obeyed, and the liberty it restores is the State's to deliver, not the prisoner's to demand.</p>

<h2>The constitutional remedy restated</h2>
<p>The judgment works through the settled line of authority — <em>Rudul Sah v. State of Bihar</em>, <em>Nilabati Behera v. State of Orissa</em>, and the distinction from <em>Kasturilal</em> — to restate the architecture of compensation for violations of Article 21.</p>

<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>Public-law remedy</h3><p>Articles 32 and 226 empower constitutional courts to award compensation for fundamental-rights violations, on principles of strict liability.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>No sovereign immunity</h3><p>The defence that fails against Article 21 claims: the State answers for its instrumentalities' failures.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Compensatory, not punitive</h3><p>The award addresses pecuniary and non-pecuniary loss — lost years, lost earnings, lost standing — aiming at the claimant's financial self-sufficiency.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Additional, not exclusive</h3><p>The constitutional award does not bar a civil suit for damages; it is a distinct, faster remedy.</p></div>
</div>

<p>On quantum, the Court found Rs 2 lakh wholly inadequate for years of unlawful incarceration following a final acquittal, and fixed Rs 30 lakh covering all heads — directing a payment structure (savings account plus auto-renewing fixed deposit) designed to protect the corpus. Copies of the judgment were directed to the Registrars General of all High Courts and to the District Legal Services Authority, so that the monitoring of post-acquittal releases is treated as an institutional duty rather than an individual's luck.</p>

<h2>What this means in practice</h2>
<div class="check">
<p><strong>For families of prisoners:</strong> an acquittal or a bail order is only half the work; its implementation must be verified with the jail authorities, and any delay documented. Where release is delayed without lawful cause, a writ petition under Article 226 seeking both release and compensation is the proper vehicle — and this judgment now anchors the quantum conversation well above token figures.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>The decision also carries a quieter lesson about legal aid: the man remained in prison partly because no one told him he was free. Effective representation does not end with the judgment — a duty the Court's circulation directions to High Courts and legal services authorities are plainly meant to institutionalise.</p>
</div>
